-- title:
--   Invertible module on an affine scheme: finite principal trivialising cover
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring and let $N$ be a module over the structure sheaf of $\operatorname{Spec} S$, i.e. an object of $(\operatorname{Spec} S).\mathrm{Modules}$. Assume `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible N`, that is: for every point $x$ of $\operatorname{Spec} S$ there is an open subscheme $U$ with $x \in U$ such that the pullback of $N$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow \operatorname{Spec} S$ admits an isomorphism to the unit module $\mathcal O_U$ on $U$ (the isomorphism type being asserted nonempty). Then there exist a natural number $k$ and elements $r : \mathrm{Fin}\,k \to S$ such that: the ideal generated by the $r_i$ is the whole of $S$; for each $i$ the pullback of $N$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the localisation map $S \to S[1/r_i] =$ `Localization.Away (r i)` is isomorphic to the unit module on $\operatorname{Spec} S[1/r_i]$; the $S$-module $\prod_{i} S[1/r_i]$ is faithfully flat; and the pullback of $N$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the map $S \to \prod_i S[1/r_i]$ is isomorphic to the unit module on $\operatorname{Spec}\bigl(\prod_i S[1/r_i]\bigr)$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that an invertible sheaf on an affine scheme is trivialised on a finite cover by basic opens, packaged together with faithful flatness of the product of the corresponding localisations and triviality over that product (so that a single faithfully flat affine base change trivialises $N$). It serves the 'reduction to the rigidifiable case' step in effective descent for polarised abelian schemes, being cited by the descent and comparison results for such schemes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_Modules_IsInvertible_exists_finite_away_cover_trivial.lean

